problems.problem:
Let $r$ be the radius of the inscribed circle of a right triangle, and let $h$ be the height of the triangle, drawn to its hypotenuse. This height divides the original triangle into two smaller triangles, let $r_1$ and $r_2$ be the radii of the circles inscribed in these triangles. Prove the following equalities:

a) $r_1 + r_2 + r = h$;

b) $r_1^2 + r_2^2 = r^2$.

Let $a$, $b$ and $c$ be the side lengths of some triangle. Prove that the following inequalities hold:

a) $2(ab + bc + ac) > a^2 + b^2 + c^2$;

b) $2(a^2b^2 + b^2c^2 + a^2c^2) > a^4 + b^4 + c^4$.

Every student of the tenth grade sends Easter greeting cards to fifteen of his or her classmates. Prove that:

a) if there are $30$ students in the class, then there certainly exist two students who send greeting cards to each other;

b) if there are $31$ students in the class, then it is possible to send the cards so that no pair of students send greeting cards to each other.

Find:

a) all quadruples of positive integers $(a, k, l, m)$ for which the equality $a^k = a^l + a^m$ holds;

b) all quintuples of positive integers $(a, k, l, m, n)$ for which the equality $a^k = a^l + a^m + a^n$ holds.

A tribe called Ababab uses only letters A and B, and they create words according to the following rules:

(1) A is a word;

(2) if $w$ is a word, then $ww$ and $w\overline{w}$ are also words, where $\overline{w}$ is obtained from $w$ by replacing all letters A with B and all letters B with A ($xy$ denotes the concatenation of $x$ and $y$);

(3) all words are created by rules (1) and (2).

Prove that any two words with the same number of letters differ exactly in half of their letters.

Do there exist $5$ different points in the plane such that all triangles with vertices at these points are right triangles and

a) no four of the chosen points lie on the same line;

b) no three of the chosen points lie on the same line?

Prove or disprove: For every integer $n > 0$, there is a polynomial $a_n x^n + a_{n-1} x^{n-1} + \ldots + a_1 x + a_0$ satisfying the following conditions:

(1) All coefficients $a_n, a_{n-1}, \ldots, a_1, a_0$ are positive real numbers;

(2) At least one of the coefficients is $\frac{1}{n}$;

(3) The value of the polynomial is integer for every integer $x$.

Natural numbers $1$ through $n$ are written on a blackboard. On each move, one erases from the blackboard $2$ or more numbers whose sum is divisible by each of the chosen numbers and writes their sum on the blackboard. Two players make moves by turns and the player who cannot move loses the game. Which player can win the game against any play by the opponent, if:

(a) $n = 6$;

(b) $n = 11$?
